// SOS M3 unit 2.75: RELEASING `NO_HANDLE` IS A FAULT (sawos design 3 D-2; the
// faults ruling, design 178).
//
//   SOS releasenothing: releasing a word that names nothing
//   SOS: process fault: bad handle process=0
//   SOS: process teardown handles=3 ... process=0
//
// Handle 0 names nothing — §3's oldest rule, and the reason a zeroed register is
// never authority — so releasing it is asking the kernel to destroy a
// capability the caller never had. That is caller-checkable in the strongest
// sense: the process chose the number. The faults ruling ends it, and it is the
// SAME `BadHandle` any other unresolvable word gets, which is the point of the
// three cases in this family: `NO_HANDLE`, a malformed word and a stale one are
// one answer, not three.
//
// THE TEARDOWN COUNT IS THE SECOND ASSERTION. Three handles — §12's boot set and
// nothing else — because this process deliberately derives nothing before it
// dies. That is what makes the number a statement about the FAULT rather than
// about whatever the program happened to be holding.
//
// ONE FAULT PER CASE, which is why this is not an arm of `handle-remint`: a
// fault ends the process, so a second probe after one would never run.

import sos.{System, sos_handle_release}

static EXIT_FAILED: UInt = 1

/// The word that names nothing, at every generation. It is `sosabi`'s
/// `NO_HANDLE`, spelled here because this file works at the C altitude where a
/// handle is a number — and because the whole claim is that a PROCESS may write
/// this number and the kernel must refuse it.
static NAMES_NOTHING: UInt = 0

@export("_start")
func _start(boot_handle: UInt) {
    let system = System(boot_handle: boot_handle)

    let _ = system.debug_print("SOS releasenothing: releasing a word that names nothing\n")

    // Does not return: the kernel ends this process for a handle that resolves
    // to no object.
    let _ = sos_handle_release(NAMES_NOTHING)

    let _ = system.debug_print("SOS releasenothing: survived, which is the bug\n")
    let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
}
